Color modes
----------------------------- RGB (Red, Green, Blue)
The RGB color model is used to define colors by mixing Red, Green, and Blue light.
Syntax:
rgb(red, green, blue)

=>Each value represents the intensity of a color component and ranges from 0 to 255:

Value	Meaning
0	No intensity (dark)
255	Full intensity (brightest)

rgb(255, 0, 0) → full red

rgb(0, 255, 0) → full green

rgb(0, 0, 255) → full blue

rgb(0, 0, 0) → black (no light)

rgb(255, 255, 255) → white (full light from all three)

The higher the values, the brighter the color. Lower values produce darker shades


------------------------------------------RGBA (Red, Green, Blue, Alpha)

RGBA works the same as RGB, but includes an additional Alpha channel for opacity (transparency).

Syntax:

rgba(red, green, blue, alpha)


red, green, blue → 0 to 255 (same as RGB)

alpha → 0 to 1

1 = fully opaque (visible)

0 = fully transparent (invisible)

0.5 = 50% transparency

Examples:

Code	Output Meaning
rgba(255, 0, 0, 1)	Fully opaque red
rgba(255, 0, 0, 0.5)	Red with 50% transparency
rgba(255, 0, 0, 0)	Fully transparent (invisible)

Alpha does not change the brightness of the color — it affects how transparent the color is when placed on a background.
